Id: stor008w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_storage
Fix: Blacklist usb-storage and uas in /etc/modprobe.d and rebuild the initramfs, or boot with usbcore.authorized_default=0 (or 2).
References: modprobe.d(5)
Controls: NIST MP-7; ISO 7.10

The policy is to allow no USB storage (Tigris_Storage_NoUSB=Y in
tigris.conf), but neither driver is blocked in /etc/modprobe.d (both
usb-storage and uas must be: USB3 sticks use uas when it loads),
and the running kernel does not leave new devices unauthorized
either (usbcore authorized_default 0, or 2 for internal devices
only; set on the kernel command line, since usbcore is usually built
in and never reads modprobe.d), so a USB stick plugged in is mounted
and data can be copied off or on. Offline, only the blacklist is
judged. The same id reports a storage device already connected.

To fix it, add to a file in /etc/modprobe.d:

    install usb-storage /bin/false
    blacklist usb-storage
    install uas /bin/false
    blacklist uas

then rebuild the initramfs (update-initramfs -u, or dracut -f). If USB
storage is wanted, set Tigris_Storage_NoUSB=N.
